PixelCanvasEffect_083126
Last reviewed: 083126

Originating Prompt: we are trying to integrate functionality similar to the
app , desktop terminal effect in python on github. he is doing something
about rendering each character space like a pixel space and its really
cool. id like to have some functionality like that in deskapp

Follow-up Prompt: here is the link for the project im talking about
https://github.com/ChrisBuilds/terminaltexteffects

Interpretation: The referenced project is TerminalTextEffects (TTE) by
ChrisBuilds. Having reviewed its README, TTE is a text/character
ANIMATION engine, not a pixel-intensity/cellular-automaton renderer like
deskapp/mods/fire.py. This changes the shape of the work versus the
original draft of this proposal, which had assumed a fire.py-style
buffer. TTE's actual model:

  - Input is real text (e.g. a banner/logo/log line), not a blank grid.
  - Each input character becomes an EffectCharacter object with a home
    coordinate (its final position in the text).
  - Characters move via Paths made of Waypoints, with motion easing
    (including bezier curves) — e.g. "characters fly in from off-canvas
    and land in their home position."
  - Characters animate symbol/color over time via Scenes (ordered frames
    changing the glyph and/or color, with easing), independent of
    motion.
  - Full Xterm-256 / 24-bit RGB color and gradients are first-class, not
    a side feature.
  - A canvas abstraction handles sizing/anchoring text within the
    terminal.
  - Effects (beams, rain, matrix, fireworks, bouncyballs, burn, smoke,
    etc.) are plugins composed from Paths + Scenes + a per-frame update
    loop, run at a target frame rate.

This is architecturally closer to a "characters as animated sprites with
paths/keyframes" engine than to fire.py's "grid of intensity values
mapped through a glyph ramp" approach. Both are valid interpretations of
"treating each character space like a pixel," but they lead to different
abstractions:

  - fire.py's model (numeric grid -> glyph ramp) suits particle/field
    simulations (fire, plasma, smoke-as-density).
  - TTE's model (text characters as objects with Path + Scene) suits
    taking DeskApp's actual on-screen text/UI content and animating it
    (e.g. a panel title flying into place, a log line decrypting in,
    text color sweeps) — arguably the more interesting fit for a UI
    framework like DeskApp, since DeskApp already has real text content
    to animate rather than fire needing invented pixels.

Revising scope: rather than choosing one model, split the work into two
independent, layerable pieces so both directions from the original
discussion are covered:

  1. PixelCanvas (grid/intensity-driven, generalizes fire.py) — kept
     from the original draft, useful for fire/plasma/smoke-style effects.
  2. CharacterMotion (TTE-inspired: home-coordinate + Path/Waypoint +
     Scene model) — new, for animating existing DeskApp text/panel
     content into place or through color changes.

Given DeskApp's `render`/`Module.write()` model already deals in
characters at (row, col) positions, CharacterMotion is likely the more
directly reusable piece for DeskApp's own panels/widgets, while
PixelCanvas remains valuable for full-screen background effects like
fire.py.

Proposal:

- [x] User confirmed: pivot to CharacterMotion only, build one concrete
      TTE-inspired effect end-to-end, plus a showcase example.
      PixelCanvas is deferred — not part of this proposal's execution.
- [x] CharacterMotion (deskapp/src/character_motion.py): implemented as
      a single focused RainReveal class rather than the fully generic
      Character/Path/Scene framework originally sketched below. This is
      a deliberate scope reduction (see Review Notes) to keep the first
      cut small, dependency-free, and directly usable:
    - [x] per-character home (row, col) + falling state, built from a
          list of text lines via SetText()
    - [x] simple linear fall (rows/second) from a random above-canvas
          start row down to the home row — no bezier/easing, matching
          the "defer curves" note below
    - [x] landed vs. falling rendering distinction (resolved glyph in
          Color once landed; random "drop" glyph in DropColor while
          falling), the closest equivalent to a Scene for this effect
    - [x] Start()/Update()/IsDone()/Render(Mod, Panel, Row, Col, Height,
          Width) tick/render API, Update() driven by wall-clock time so
          it's frame-rate independent, called once per page() frame
    - [ ] generic reusable Path/Waypoint/Scene types decoupled from a
          single effect — not built; would be the natural next step if
          more TTE-style effects (beams, wipe, slide) are wanted later
- [x] One built-in effect: "rain" (TTE's own name for this effect) —
      applied to a banner/logo text block in the new example module
      below, as the proof of concept
- [x] Add examples/ex_character_motion.py demonstrating RainReveal
      end-to-end (banner falls into place, R/ENTER replays), consistent
      with existing examples/ex_*.py conventions
- [x] Export RainReveal from deskapp/__init__.py alongside CoreWidgets
- [ ] Update README.md's Core API section — deferred; README not
      touched in this pass, can follow in a small follow-up if desired
- [x] No changes to deskapp/mods/apt.py, about.py, buttons.py,
      hunter.py, memory_viewer.py, fire.py, or unrelated core modules
- [x] Roll version to 0.10.0 and update .github/changelog.ai on
      completion, crediting Proposal 10 and this project as inspiration
      (TerminalTextEffects by ChrisBuilds, MIT licensed, no code copied —
      only the conceptual model referenced)

Original (pre-pivot) full sketch, kept for reference / possible future
follow-up proposal:

- [ ] PixelCanvas (deskapp/src/pixel_canvas.py):
    - [ ] width/height grid of numeric cell values (0-9 or 0-255
          intensity scale)
    - [ ] configurable character ramp (glyphs dark to bright, generalized
          from Flames.chars / og_chars)
    - [ ] optional per-cell color/attr mapping, separate from glyph
          selection
    - [ ] Render(Mod, Panel, Row, Col, Height, Width) writing through
          Module.write(), matching ScrollList/ResultGrid's calling
          convention in deskapp/src/widgets.py
    - [ ] refactor deskapp/mods/fire.py's Flames class onto it without
          changing fire's visible behavior
- [ ] Generic CharacterMotion Path/Waypoint/Scene framework (rather than
      the single RainReveal effect actually built):
    - [ ] a Character type: symbol, home (row, col), current position,
          current color/attr
    - [ ] a Path type: ordered list of waypoints (row, col) a character
          moves through, with simple linear or step-based interpolation
          per frame (defer bezier/easing curves to a later proposal)
    - [ ] a Scene type: ordered list of (symbol, color, duration) frames
          applied to a character over time, independent of its Path
    - [ ] a small per-frame update/tick step that advances all
          characters' Paths and Scenes and renders them via
          Module.write()

Review Notes:
083126 — Link reviewed (github.com/ChrisBuilds/terminaltexteffects).
Confirmed the reference project is a character-animation engine
(Paths/Waypoints/Scenes), not a pixel-intensity grid. Split proposal into
PixelCanvas (original fire.py-style idea) and CharacterMotion (new,
TTE-inspired) so the user can choose scope. Awaiting user decision on
which piece(s) to build before implementation begins.

083126 (later same day) — User confirmed: pivot to CharacterMotion only,
pick one particular TTE effect, and build a showcase example. Chose
"rain" (TTE's own name) since it's the simplest clear demonstration of
the home-coordinate + motion + reveal idea, and implemented it as a
focused RainReveal class rather than the full generic Path/Scene
framework sketched during proposal drafting — the generic framework adds
real complexity (interpolation, per-character scene sequencing, waypoint
lists) that isn't justified until a second/third effect is actually
needed. Documented the reduced scope explicitly above rather than
silently under-delivering against the original checklist. PixelCanvas
remains untouched and un-promoted; it can become its own proposal later
if wanted.
